#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Verified bootstrap installer for the canonical Faber dev-kit payload.

faber-onboarding Stage 3, unit A1 (delivery-stage3.md Batch A). The primary
channel installs the canonical Stage 2 release payload: the dev-kit archive
for the platform triple, SHA-256 verified against the published checksum asset
BEFORE any payload is unpacked or executed (install-channel-matrix.md checksum
policy). A mismatch aborts with no partial install — nothing under the prefix
is touched before verification passes.

This is a shell bootstrap, NOT a second release system (CAMPAIGN Stage 3
overlap rule): it downloads a fixed release payload (archive name
`faber-v<version>-<triple>.tar.gz` + basename-only `.sha256`, release-contract
.md §5.1) and installs the dev-kit layout (dev-kit-contract.md):

    <prefix>/bin/faber
    <prefix>/share/faber/reference/
    <prefix>/share/faber/locale/<locale>/pack.toml

Prefix rules (install-channel-matrix.md): default is the user-local prefix
`~/.faber` (no sudo, no hidden writes); explicit `--prefix` covers system and
agent installs. Agent installs are non-interactive: no prompts ever, stable
exit codes, human-readable change report (the JSON diagnostics surface is
faber Stage 9 — labeled staged, not implemented here).

Reinstall idempotency matrix (Stage 3 A2): a same-version re-run reports
"already current" and changes nothing; missing or tampered payload files are
restored and reported; installing a DIFFERENT version over the same prefix
fails closed and names the upgrade path (`faber self update`).

`--update` performs a versioned install on the existing install (the `faber
self update` engine): an upgrade to a newer version OR a pinned downgrade to
an older version runs through the same side-by-side lane machinery
(`<prefix>/versions/<version>/`). The current install is preserved as a lane
first, the target version is installed as its own lane, and the active
launcher/packs/receipt flip to the target version with rollback. User
projects and the package store (`<prefix>/cistae`, per
package-and-lock-contract.md) are never touched. Update/downgrade failures
fail closed: a checksum mismatch aborts before any write, and a failed write
rolls back, leaving the previously active version unchanged.

Downgrade policy (Stage 3 A3): a pinned downgrade to an older version is
supported and reversible through the lane layout. It FAILS CLOSED on
incompatible packs — a downgrade that would strand a locked pack of a user
project (a `faber.lock` under `<prefix>/projects/` whose locked package's
`faber_min` compatibility bound the target cannot satisfy, per
package-and-lock-contract.md OQ3/OQ6) is rejected with a typed cause
(`incompatible-pack-downgrade`), the dev-kit layer, and ONE next action —
never a partial or silent mix of versions. Cross-lane version changes (odd
development major <-> even LTS major, faber/docs/release/policy.md) still
require the explicit `--allow-lane-change` flag.

Destination-side containment (CTO audit FINDING 1): every write beneath the
prefix — payload, rollback, receipt — is proven to stay within the canonical
prefix WITHOUT following a pre-existing symlink. A pre-existing
`prefix/share -> /outside` symlink is rejected before any write; both sides
stay byte-identical. The same rule applies to PATH-owned writes (the shell rc
append must not follow a symlink out of the user's home).

Exit codes:
  0  installed, updated, downgraded, or already current (idempotent re-run)
  1  checksum mismatch, version conflict (cross-version without --update,
     cross-lane without --allow-lane-change), incompatible locked pack set
     on a downgrade, or install/update failure (fail-closed; no partial
     install)
  2  usage error (unsupported platform/triple, malformed arguments)

Proxy/offline: downloads go through urllib and honor the standard
HTTP_PROXY / HTTPS_PROXY / http_proxy / https_proxy environment variables.
Offline or missing assets fail closed with exit 1 and a message naming the
URL. `--base-url` may point at a mirror or a local directory (release-contract
.md §7 defers mirrors; the checksum is still verified against the .sha256
fetched from that same source).

Curl convenience path (self-contained; needs only python3 — no repo checkout).
When the script itself is published at the release tag (release interlock R1):

    curl -fsSL https://github.com/faberlang/releases/releases/download/\\
        faber-v1.6.0/install-faber | python3 - --version 1.6.0

Verification of the installed kit is a separate step that reuses the Stage 2
verifier against the installed prefix:

    scripta/verify-dev-kit --payload <prefix> --version <version>

Usage:
    scripta/install-faber [--version 1.6.0] [--prefix ~/.faber]
        [--triple aarch64-apple-darwin] [--base-url <url|dir>] [--add-to-path]
    scripta/install-faber --version 1.6.1 --prefix ~/.faber --update   # upgrade
    faber self update --version 1.6.1                                  # same engine
"""

